When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:

- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.

- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.

For example, let's find $AB,$ where 
<div class="matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked">
<div>$A=\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 & 3\end{bmatrix}$</div>
</div>
and
$\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\ 2 & -2 \\ -4 & 1\end{bmatrix}$

First, we verify the conformability for multiplication:

$$
\underset{\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \times \color{green}{\fbox{3}}}{A} \underset{\color{green}\checkmark}{\cdot} \underset{{\color{green}\fbox{3}} \times \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = 
	\underset{\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \times \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{C}
$$

So the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:

$$
\begin{aligned}
	\underbrace{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -2 & 2 & 1  \\
	1 & -1 & 3 
    \end{bmatrix}}_{A}

	\underbrace{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -5 & 2   \\
	2 & -2   \\
	-4 & 1 
    \end{bmatrix}}_{B}

	&= 
	\underbrace{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \ast & \ast   \\
        \ast & \ast 
    \end{bmatrix}
	}_{C}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\ast$'s in the equation above).

To find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the $1$st row and $1$st column, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{blue}-2 & \color{blue}2 & \color{blue}1  \\
	\color{lightgray}1 & \color{lightgray}-1 & \color{lightgray}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
         \color{red}-5 & \color{lightgray}2   \\
	\color{red}2 & \color{lightgray}-2   \\
	\color{red}-4 & \color{lightgray}1
    \end{bmatrix}

\%	&= 
\%    \left[
\%   \begin{matrix}
\%       ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}}) & \ast   \\
\%        \ast & \ast
\%    \end{matrix}
\%    \right]

	= \begin{bmatrix}
        \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} & \ast   \\
        \ast & \ast
    \end{bmatrix}, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} = ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}})
\end{aligned}
$$

To find $c_{12},$ we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{blue}-2 & \color{blue}2 & \color{blue}1  \\
	\color{lightgray}1 & \color{lightgray}-1 & \color{lightgray}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{lightgray}-5 & \color{red}2   \\
	\color{lightgray}2 & \color{red}-2   \\
	\color{lightgray}-4 & \color{red}1
    \end{bmatrix}

\%	&= 
\%    \left[
\%    \begin{matrix}
\%        10 & ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}   \\
\%        \ast & \ast
\%    \end{matrix}
\%    \right]

	= \begin{bmatrix}
        10 & \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7}   \\
        \ast & \ast
    \end{bmatrix}, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7} = ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
\end{aligned}
$$

To find $c_{21},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{lightgray}-2 & \color{lightgray}2 & \color{lightgray}1  \\
	\color{blue}1 & \color{blue}-1 & \color{blue}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
         \color{red}-5 & \color{lightgray}2   \\
	\color{red}2 & \color{lightgray}-2   \\
	\color{red}-4 & \color{lightgray}1
    \end{bmatrix}

\%	&= 
\%    \left[
\%    \begin{matrix}
\%        10 & -7   \\
\%        {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}}) & \ast
\%    \end{matrix}
\%    \right]

	= \begin{bmatrix}
        10 & -7   \\
        \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} & \ast
    \end{bmatrix}, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} = {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}})
\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, to find $c_{22},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{lightgray}-2 & \color{lightgray}2 & \color{lightgray}1  \\
	\color{blue}1 & \color{blue}-1 & \color{blue}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{lightgray}-5 & \color{red}2   \\
	\color{lightgray}2 & \color{red}-2   \\
	\color{lightgray}-4 & \color{red}1
    \end{bmatrix}

\%	&= 
\%    \left[
\%    \begin{matrix}
\%        10 & -7   \\
\%        -19 & {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
\%    \end{matrix}
\%    \right]

	= \begin{bmatrix}
        10 & -7   \\
        -19 & \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7}
    \end{bmatrix}, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7} = {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we have
$\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\ -19 & 7\end{bmatrix}$
